WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.200
Welcome back to Building
Ticket-to-Machine.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.118
This is Chapter 7: evidence
records for the Federal

3
00:00:06.118 --> 00:00:09.663
Modernization Workbook's
Book 13 and the I S S O.

4
00:00:09.663 --> 00:00:12.325
The I S S O doesn't need the tracker.

5
00:00:12.325 --> 00:00:15.679
The I S S O needs one record
per finding, in one shape,

6
00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:19.850
that nobody could have edited
afterwards without it showing.

7
00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:23.170
This chapter reads closed
findings out of ServiceNow,

8
00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:27.744
writes each in Book 13's evidence shape,
checks what an assessor would check,

9
00:00:27.744 --> 00:00:31.137
and packages the period with
a hash chain and a verifier.

10
00:00:31.137 --> 00:00:33.850
Then two labs and the dangers.

11
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.575
Why records rather than the tracker?

12
00:00:39.575 --> 00:00:42.900
Because an assessor asks different
questions from an operator.

13
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.825
Chapter 5's re-read client already writes
evidence onto every record it closes,

14
00:00:47.825 --> 00:00:50.325
but nothing read those records back out.

15
00:00:50.325 --> 00:00:54.870
And the assessor's worries are specific:
was a record edited after it closed,

16
00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.362
so it now says something
the re-read didn't?

17
00:00:57.362 --> 00:01:01.174
Was one quietly deleted, so
the period only looks complete?

18
00:01:01.174 --> 00:01:05.787
Were fields filled in to claim an approval
or an execution that never happened?

19
00:01:05.787 --> 00:01:08.160
This chapter answers each one.

20
00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:16.548
Each closed finding becomes one
record with Book 13's seven fields.

21
00:01:16.548 --> 00:01:19.610
Origin: when and where
the drift was detected.

22
00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:25.097
Owner and clock: who, how urgent, when it
was due and whether it finished in time.

23
00:01:25.097 --> 00:01:28.861
The result: the re-read evidence
and whether it's intact.

24
00:01:28.861 --> 00:01:30.798
And the closure, with its lane.

25
00:01:30.798 --> 00:01:35.977
The other three fields, approval,
the executing identity and what ran,

26
00:01:35.977 --> 00:01:40.610
are written as null, each with its
reason: Pass 1 doesn't actuate.

27
00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.676
The record says what it doesn't hold,

28
00:01:42.676 --> 00:01:45.950
rather than leaving the
field out or filling it in.

29
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.125
Every record gets four
checks, each reported by name.

30
00:01:53.125 --> 00:01:56.850
Is the evidence intact: does it
still hash to the stored hash?

31
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.770
An edit after closure fails,
and so does a value cut

32
00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.762
short by the field's
four-thousand-character limit.

33
00:02:02.762 --> 00:02:04.126
Is there evidence at all?

34
00:02:04.126 --> 00:02:06.950
A close that bypassed
the business rule fails.

35
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.638
Did the evidence say the
intended state matched?

36
00:02:09.638 --> 00:02:11.551
And did it finish inside its window?

37
00:02:11.551 --> 00:02:16.868
Window adherence is measured as closures
inside the window over closures judged,

38
00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:21.050
not a ratio that improves just
because new findings arrive.

39
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:25.925
Then the package.

40
00:02:25.925 --> 00:02:30.057
Each line of the records file carries
the hash of the line before it,

41
00:02:30.057 --> 00:02:33.287
so removing or reordering
a record breaks the chain.

42
00:02:33.287 --> 00:02:37.175
The excerpt on the slide is
the whole idea in seven lines.

43
00:02:37.175 --> 00:02:39.524
A summary file holds window adherence,

44
00:02:39.524 --> 00:02:42.900
the declared exceptions and
the records with problems.

45
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.900
And a manifest holds a hash of
every file and the chain's head.

46
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.886
Next month's export continues the
chain from this month's manifest,

47
00:02:50.886 --> 00:02:55.530
and a fixed timestamp means the same
records always give the same bytes.

48
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.430
Two more pieces.

49
00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:04.418
A verifier recomputes every
file's hash and walks the chain;

50
00:03:04.418 --> 00:03:08.405
that's the check an I S S O runs
on a package someone hands them.

51
00:03:08.405 --> 00:03:13.147
And a live read pulls closed and
cancelled findings through the Table API,

52
00:03:13.147 --> 00:03:18.355
under its own read-only role from Chapter
2, so the export can never close anything.

53
00:03:18.355 --> 00:03:23.448
Five fictional records cover the cases:
one closed in its window, one closed late,

54
00:03:23.448 --> 00:03:26.380
one whose evidence was
edited after it closed,

55
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:31.590
one cancelled as a declared exception,
and one closed with no evidence at all.

56
00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.118
Eight tests cover the export: the seven
fields and the null later-pass fields;

57
00:03:40.118 --> 00:03:44.895
the late, tampered and unevidenced
closures and a truncated evidence field;

58
00:03:44.895 --> 00:03:48.611
the package and its verification,
with tampering detected;

59
00:03:48.611 --> 00:03:52.175
the chain continued across
periods; reproducible bytes;

60
00:03:52.175 --> 00:03:55.815
and evidence written by the
real re-read client verifying.

61
00:03:55.815 --> 00:03:59.890
One honest limit: the package
is tamper-evident, not signed.

62
00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:04.627
Anyone who can rewrite every file and
the manifest can rewrite the chain.

63
00:04:04.627 --> 00:04:09.070
Signing, and where the package
lives, are the program's decisions.

64
00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:17.075
The vendor facts are few: the Table
API's read and its query parameters,

65
00:04:17.075 --> 00:04:21.628
ServiceNow's Government Community
Cloud at FedRAMP High, and SailPoint's

66
00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:25.957
Search events index, which the SailPoint
half will read when it's built.

67
00:04:25.957 --> 00:04:29.514
The record shape, the window
and the lanes are Book 13's,

68
00:04:29.514 --> 00:04:32.020
and the hash chain is design guidance.

69
00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:37.253
Two points stay open: the format and time
zone of the dates the Table API returns,

70
00:04:37.253 --> 00:04:39.507
which the export parses both ways,

71
00:04:39.507 --> 00:04:43.532
and what SailPoint keeps in its
events index and for how long.

72
00:04:43.532 --> 00:04:46.050
That's why the SailPoint half waits.

73
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.188
This chapter's output is the evidence.

74
00:04:52.188 --> 00:04:56.600
One record per finding supports change
control and continuous monitoring.

75
00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.294
Records unchanged after
closure, and none removed,

76
00:05:00.294 --> 00:05:06.275
supports protection of audit information,
AU-9, through the manifest and the chain.

77
00:05:06.275 --> 00:05:11.762
Closures inside the windows, with the late
ones named, goes to the governance board.

78
00:05:11.762 --> 00:05:15.400
Declared exceptions carry their
note and a plan of action item.

79
00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.063
And what isn't evidenced
yet is stated outright.

80
00:05:18.063 --> 00:05:20.994
Non-repudiation isn't in
the Moderate baseline;

81
00:05:20.994 --> 00:05:24.060
signing the package
would be a further step.

82
00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:28.860
Who's involved?

83
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.777
Developers who build the evidence
pipeline, and the I S S O and

84
00:05:32.777 --> 00:05:37.635
assessors who read what it produces
and verify every package they receive.

85
00:05:37.635 --> 00:05:41.249
The export runs as its own
identity: the evidence reader,

86
00:05:41.249 --> 00:05:46.119
with read-only access to the finding
table, its own user and OAuth client, and

87
00:05:46.119 --> 00:05:51.147
never the verifier's role, because it must
not be able to close what it's hashing.

88
00:05:51.147 --> 00:05:55.132
And keep each period's manifest
somewhere the exporter can't write,

89
00:05:55.132 --> 00:05:57.510
signed if the program requires it.

90
00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.072
Lab 1.

91
00:06:02.072 --> 00:06:03.348
Eight tests pass.

92
00:06:03.348 --> 00:06:06.611
Then export October's
five fictional findings.

93
00:06:06.611 --> 00:06:10.310
Four closed on a re-read, and
one is a declared exception.

94
00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:13.348
Two of three judged closures
were inside the window.

95
00:06:13.348 --> 00:06:16.304
Then the problems, by
name: one closed late,

96
00:06:16.304 --> 00:06:21.696
one whose evidence no longer matches
its hash, and one closed with no re-read

97
00:06:21.696 --> 00:06:24.914
evidence at all; its
close note just says done,

98
00:06:24.914 --> 00:06:27.697
which Book 13 calls prose, not evidence.

99
00:06:27.697 --> 00:06:32.161
The command exits with a failure rather
than producing a clean-looking package.

100
00:06:32.161 --> 00:06:34.410
The last line is the chain's head.

101
00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.634
Now act as the I S S O.

102
00:06:39.634 --> 00:06:45.923
Verify the package: every file matches
the manifest and the chain is unbroken.

103
00:06:45.923 --> 00:06:48.711
Start November's chain
from October's head;

104
00:06:48.711 --> 00:06:52.935
November's manifest records
October's head as its previous link.

105
00:06:52.935 --> 00:06:58.035
The lab reuses October's records for
this, where a real November has its own.

106
00:06:58.035 --> 00:07:02.135
Then remove one record from
October's package and verify again.

107
00:07:02.135 --> 00:07:05.689
Two problems: the file's hash
no longer matches the manifest,

108
00:07:05.689 --> 00:07:09.098
and the next line's link points
at a record that isn't there.

109
00:07:09.098 --> 00:07:11.070
The package doesn't hold.

110
00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.142
Lab 2 exports for real from
your developer instance,

111
00:07:18.142 --> 00:07:22.933
with at least one finding closed in Chapter
5's lab; the authors haven't run it.

112
00:07:22.933 --> 00:07:25.707
Create the evidence reader
and check its grant.

113
00:07:25.707 --> 00:07:28.602
Export live since the date
of your test closure,

114
00:07:28.602 --> 00:07:31.570
and expect every record
intact with no problems.

115
00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:35.763
Check that the due and finished
times are the ones you expect,

116
00:07:35.763 --> 00:07:39.620
in UTC, since the Table API's
date format wasn't confirmed.

117
00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:41.108
Verify the package.

118
00:07:41.108 --> 00:07:45.260
Then delete the output and the
reader's client, user and role.

119
00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:51.910
The dangers map onto
the assessor's worries.

120
00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:55.885
An edit after closure is caught
by recomputing the evidence hash.

121
00:07:55.885 --> 00:07:59.016
Evidence cut short by the
field limit is reported,

122
00:07:59.016 --> 00:08:01.460
and the attachment keeps the full copy.

123
00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:05.685
A close that bypassed the business
rule shows as closed without evidence.

124
00:08:05.685 --> 00:08:07.797
A deleted record breaks the chain.

125
00:08:07.797 --> 00:08:12.051
A package rewritten end to end is
the one the chain alone can't catch,

126
00:08:12.051 --> 00:08:15.222
so store manifests where
the exporter can't write.

127
00:08:15.222 --> 00:08:18.472
Fields filled to look
complete are null by design.

128
00:08:18.472 --> 00:08:21.590
And the export never runs as the verifier.

129
00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:26.540
Done looks like this.

130
00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:30.865
The export runs on a schedule as the
evidence reader and holds nothing more.

131
00:08:30.865 --> 00:08:35.328
Each period's package verifies and
continues the last period's chain.

132
00:08:35.328 --> 00:08:38.017
Every reported problem
has gone to its owner,

133
00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:40.489
and none sits in the package unexplained.

134
00:08:40.489 --> 00:08:43.890
Manifests are stored where the
exporter can't overwrite them.

135
00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:47.840
Window adherence is reviewed
with Book 13's other measures.

136
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.253
And the tests pass.

137
00:08:49.253 --> 00:08:52.652
Next, Chapter 8: one writer per attribute.

138
00:08:52.652 --> 00:08:53.800
Thanks for watching.
